<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umbria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umbria is a landlocked region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> valley. It takes its name from the ancient Umbrians, and its hill towns, among them Perugia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and Orvieto, were long part of the Papal States before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860. Assisi made it the homeland of Saint Francis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umbria.jpg" alt="Umbria" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umbria_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That May Giuseppe Garibaldi’s Expedition of the Thousand landed in Sicily and overthrew the Bourbons in the south, and Sardinian troops marched through the Papal Marche and <b>Umbria</b> to meet him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Campagna was given over to great estates and grazing, increasingly malarial and thinly populated, while the Marche and <b>Umbria</b> grew grain, wine and olives, and Bologna, the richest city of the state, had a large silk industry and Europe’s oldest university.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Arretium, Etruscan Aritim, modern Arezzo, lay where the Val di Chiana meets the upper Arno, controlling routes between Etruria, <b>Umbria</b> and the Po valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/arretium">Arretium</a></li><li><blockquote>Though within the lands claimed by the Papal States, it governed itself and conquered much of central <b>Umbria</b>, including rival Assisi and Foligno.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-perugia">Commune of Perugia</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy grew into a large territory covering much of <b>Umbria</b>, the Marche and Abruzzo, largely independent of the kings in Pavia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-spoleto">Duchy of Spoleto</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 Gian Galeazzo bought the title of duke from Emperor Wenceslas, turning the lordship into the Duchy of Milan, which he expanded towards Tuscany and <b>Umbria</b> before his death in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
